===================
Type Checker README
===================

To build:

  $ make install && make

The type checker is part of the compilation process, so to type check a file,
you can run:

  $ node build/phase1/main-wrapper.js -type-check --run path/to/file.arr

Note that the --run flag is necessary for now, so as to use the new module system.

Tests
-----

  $ make type-check-test

runs two suites:

1. tests/type-check/main.arr — the original directory-based runner. Every
   .arr file in tests/type-check/good/ must compile (with type-check on) and
   run without errors; every file in tests/type-check/bad/ must produce at
   least one type error.

2. tests/type-check/main2.arr — check-block suites in
   tests/type-check/tests/test-tc-*.arr, one file per feature area. These
   compile inline program strings in-process via tests/type-check/tc-helper.arr,
   so hundreds of programs check in one run without per-file process startup.
   Unlike the bad/ directory runner, they assert *which* CompileError variant
   each bad program produces.

The helper's main entry points:

  msgs(prog)                rendered type errors for a one-module program
                            (empty list means it type-checks; test failures
                            print the actual errors)
  prog is%(tc-err) pred     the program's errors all satisfy pred
                            (e.g. CS.is-type-mismatch)
  errs-with / msgs-with / tc-err-with
                            same, with a list of {name; source} dependency
                            modules resolved in memory

Serialized module boundary
--------------------------

Provided types are serialized into a module's compiled -static.js artifact
and re-inflated (src/js/base/type-util.js, then provides-from-raw-provides in
compile-structs.arr) when a dependent module compiles against the cache. Bugs
here don't show up in single-process compiles, where dependencies' provides
stay in memory.

tests/type-check/tests/test-tc-serialized.arr covers this: the helper's
compile-serialized-twice writes the modules under
tests/type-check/tmp-serialized/, compiles the entry module twice against an
on-disk cache, and returns both error lists. The first compile sees its
dependencies' types in memory; the second re-reads them from the serialized
artifacts (the entry module itself is never cached). A difference between the
two runs is a round-trip bug.
